This change makes `IntegTestCase` always include its own stack in the integ test manifest. By doing this, we create a natural stack to hook assertions (using `cdk-deplossert`) to:

```ts
/***************** System under test *************************/
const stackUnderTest = new Stack(app, 'sut');
const queue = new sqs.Queue(stackUnderTest, 'MyQueue', {
  maxMessageSizeBytes: 2048,
});
/*************************************************************/


/***************** Integ test infrastructure *****************/
// This stack will always be deployed by integ-runner. 
// If you want to make sure the assertions will run, pass this stack to Deplossert
const integrationTestStack = new Stack(app, 'integrationTestStack');
new IntegTestCase(integrationTestStack, 'test', {
  stacks: [stackUnderTest],
});
/*************************************************************/


/******************** Assertions *****************************/
const deplossert = new Deplossert(app, {
  stack: integrationTestStack
});

const aws = deplossert.queryAws({
  service: 'SQS',
  api: 'getQueueAttributes',
  parameters: {
    QueueUrl: queue.queueUrl,
    AttributeNames: ['MaximumMessageSize'],
  },
});

aws.assertEqual({
  Attributes: {
    MaximumMessageSize: '2048',
  },
});

aws.assertEqual({
  Attributes: {
    MaximumMessageSize: '1024',
  },
});
/*************************************************************/

```

In the future, we can create a sort of construct sugar, to hide some of these details. Something like:

```ts
new HigherLevelConstructForIntegTestAndAssertions(this, 'test', {
	stacks: [stackUnderTest],
	diffAssets: false,
	... // any other properties from TestCase
    assertions: (deplossert: Deplossert) => {
	    const aws = deplossert.queryAws(...);
		aws.assertEqual(...);
    },
});
```

This construct would have to live outside the CDK.

